        "title": "Who needs sleep?",
        "text": "It is 2 am. The time when you should be in bed, fast asleep. But open the curtains and you may be surprised at how many lights are on in your street. Night-time is no longer only for sleeping. It has become the new daytime, giving us the chance to catch up on everything we could not fit into what used to be our waking hours. Now, instead of sleeping, we can check our bank balance by phone, buy groceries, search the internet for cheap flights or go to the gym.\n\nBut this flexibility comes at a price. Our bodies are run by circadian rhythms, a prehistoric internal clock that controls when we feel sleepy or awake and affects our body temperature and how alert we are. It makes our brains and bodies active during the day and lets them recover during the night. This clock is so strong that even two weeks of night shifts without a break will not destroy its natural rhythm. When scientists keep volunteers in isolation, with no clues about what time of day it is, they still show daily cycles of temperature change, sleep and wakefulness, and hormone release. Constantly working against our body's natural rhythm is likely to cause us both physical and psychological damage and, research shows, may actually increase our risk of health problems such as stomach ulcers.\n\nConsultant Tom Mackey believes that our normal circadian rhythms are being more and more seriously disturbed. \"More and more of us are under pressure to do things at odd hours. This is going to affect the quality and length of our sleep. If people don't go to bed at a reasonable time, say around 11 pm, and get between six and eight hours of sleep, they will be unable to concentrate and likely to have mood swings. You need sleep for rest and repair. If you overload your mind with information for too long, everything becomes disorganised, and you cannot manage your daytime activities.\"\n\nThe circadian rhythms that control the sleep–wake cycle are as old as evolution itself. Our prehistoric ancestors would have needed their biological clock to get them out hunting during the day, and probably into bed around nightfall to avoid predators. Our night vision is not as good as that of nocturnal animals, so our natural rhythm was to sleep when the sun went down. The invention of the electric light obviously changed that. Like most biological systems, circadian rhythms are not made to measure. Our internal clock runs a little longer than 24 hours, which is where its Latin name, circadian, comes from: it means \"about a day\".\n\nIt has been known for centuries that people's circadian rhythms tend to differ. Some people are born larks (they get up early and go to bed early), while others are owls (they get up late and go to bed late). Larks are \"morning people\", ready to talk from the moment they open their eyes, while owls are grumpy and sleepy and find life impossible until midday. Research suggests that owls may pay for this with health problems, because they tend to go to sleep at less regular times. Body temperature should fall at night (and it does, whether or not we are asleep, reaching its lowest point at around 4 am), but it falls further in larks than in owls, which gives larks better quality sleep.\n\nMuch of the damage that can be done by disturbing the biological clock has been seen in people who work night shifts. The tiredness and confusion that follow night shifts are similar to those of jet lag. As with jet lag, the body adjusts over time, but the sleep–wake cycle never seems to be completely reversed. As more of us push back our circadian clocks, going to bed later and later, the effects on our bodies will be similar to those on night-shift workers, and that cannot be good."
